# <%= current_page.data.title %>

## Purpose

[cnp-jenkins-library](https://github.com/hmcts/cnp-jenkins-library) is a
[Jenkins shared library](https://www.jenkins.io/doc/book/pipeline/shared-libraries/)
that defines the HMCTS [common pipeline](../common-pipeline/common-pipeline.html):
an opinionated Continuous Delivery pipeline that builds, tests and deploys code
to production while enforcing a standard set of checks — unit tests, security
checks, Sonar scanning, container builds, smoke and functional tests.

## When to use it

Use this library if your repository builds on HMCTS Jenkins. It provides:

- `withPipeline(type, product, component)` — the opinionated application
  pipeline for `java`, `nodejs`, `angular` and `python` services
- `withInfraPipeline(product)` — the opinionated infrastructure pipeline for
  Terraform-only repositories, such as shared infrastructure
- `withNightlyPipeline(type, product, component)` — the nightly pipeline for
  scheduled tests such as security scans, fortify and performance tests

Before using it, your repository must be
[added to Jenkins](../new-component/jenkins-repository.html).

## How to import it

Jenkins is already configured with this library under the name
`Infrastructure`, so there is nothing to install. Import it at the top of your
`Jenkinsfile_CNP`:

```groovy
#!groovy

@Library("Infrastructure") _

def type = "java"        // "java", "nodejs", "angular" or "python"
def product = "rhubarb"
def component = "recipe-backend"

withPipeline(type, product, component) {
  enableSlackNotifications('#my-team-builds')
}
```

The application pipeline deploys branches to environments as follows:

| Branch | Environment |
| --- | --- |
| `master` | `aat` then `prod` |
| `demo` | `demo` |
| `perftest` | `perftest` |
| PR branch | `preview` |

The full set of options — Key Vault secrets, high level data setup, API gateway
tests, pact contract testing and more — is documented in the
[library README](https://github.com/hmcts/cnp-jenkins-library#readme).

## Versioning

`@Library("Infrastructure")` tracks the library's `master` branch, so pipelines
pick up new features and fixes automatically. This is the recommended way to
consume it.

You can pin to a release tag if you need to:

```groovy
@Library("Infrastructure@2.5.1") _
```

Pinned repositories do not receive new pipeline features, fixes or security
updates until the pin is updated. A pin can be useful to hold a known-good
version briefly — for example while a breaking library change is resolved —
but treat it as temporary and prefer tracking `master`.

To test an unmerged library change, reference the branch instead:

```groovy
@Library("Infrastructure@my-feature-branch") _
```

Branches must first be added to the
[allowed library branches list](https://github.com/hmcts/cnp-jenkins-library/blob/master/resources/uk/gov/hmcts/library/allowed-library-branches.yml)
and that change merged to `master`, otherwise the pipeline will fail.

